messages.body_plain:
I’m fitting a multinomial (by means of Poisson) IRT/ideal point model for category counts in policy documents with

Count(document, category) ~ Poisson(exp(alpha_doc + psi_cat + beta_cat*theta_doc))

As constraints, one psi and one beta are set to zero, and the ideal points theta receive a standard normal prior. A conditional statement based on the order of two betas is used to reverse the scale when necessary (I know that half-normal priors might be better for that, but I had mixed experiences when working with those).

It seems that the constraints are sufficient to identify the model, at least I get very similar results across different runs. It seems to work since the standard normal prior on the ideal points theta apparently leads to a standard normal posterior distribution of the ideal points. Is there a simple answer why this happens? I would not necessarily have expected that, and when I code the same model in Jags, theta does not come out standardized in the posterior and the model lacks identification. Is it ok to rely on the prior doing the job in Stan or would it still be better to standardize the ideal points deterministically (probably at the expense of speed?)? Any hints appreciated.

Code is as follows:

data {
int<lower=1> dir[2]; // doc id pair to set direction
int<lower=1> D; // number of docs
int<lower=1> T; // number of terms/categories
int<lower=1> N; // number of obs = number of docs * number of cats
int<lower=1> doc[N]; // doc id
int<lower=1> term[N]; // cat id
int<lower=0> y[N]; // counts as one long vector
}

parameters {
vector<lower=-20, upper=20>[D] alpha;
vector[T-1] psi_rest; // unconstrained elements of psi
vector[D] theta;
vector[T-1] beta_raw; // unconstrained elements of beta
real<lower=0> tau_psi;
real<lower=0> tau_beta;
}

transformed parameters {
vector[T] psi;
vector[T] beta;
real<lower=0> sigma_psi;
real<lower=0> sigma_beta;
psi[1] <- 0; // first cat. as reference cat.
beta[1] <- 0;
for (t in 2:T){
psi[t] <- psi_rest[t-1];
if (beta_raw[dir[2]-1]-beta_raw[dir[1]-1] > 0) {
beta[t] <- beta_raw[t-1];
}
else {
beta[t] <- -beta_raw[t-1];
}
}
sigma_beta <- 1/sqrt(tau_beta);
sigma_psi <- 1/sqrt(tau_psi);
}

model {
vector[N] logmu;
// alpha ~ uniform(-20,20);  implicit above
psi_rest ~ normal(0,sigma_psi);
beta_raw ~ normal(0,sigma_beta);
theta ~ normal(0,1);
tau_beta ~ gamma(.01,.01);
tau_psi ~ gamma(.01,.01);
for(n in 1:N){
logmu[n] <- alpha[doc[n]] + psi[term[n]] + theta[doc[n]] * beta[term[n]];
}
y ~ poisson_log(logmu) ;
}

Dear Stan Users,

I have another Stan newbies question. I am trying to fit a hierarchical logistic regression,  following the "election88_full.stan" example, but it takes more than 2 hours for iter=100 and chains=2. Most probably something is wrong with my syntax, but i cant figure out what is it. Any ideas what I might be doing wrong?  My code is below.

Thank you!

micro_code <- '

data {
int<lower=0> N;
int<lower=0> n_cnt;
int<lower=3> n_g;
int<lower=5> n_lag;
int<lower=0, upper=n_lag> lag [N];
int<lower= 1 > n_gdp;
int<lower=0,upper=n_cnt> cnt[N];
int<lower=0, upper=n_g>r0g [N];
int<lower=1, upper=34>rgdp[N];
int<lower=0,upper=97> age [N];
vector<lower=0,upper=1> [N] gender;
int<lower=1,upper=10>  income [N];
int<lower=1,upper=3>  class [N];
int<lower=1,upper=7>  lr [N];
int<lower=0,upper=1> y[N];
}

parameters {
vector[n_lag] a;
vector[n_g] b;
vector[n_gdp] c;
vector[n_cnt] d;
vector[7] beta;
real<lower=0,upper=100> sigma_a;
  real<lower=0,upper=100> sigma_b;
  real<lower=0,upper=100> sigma_c;
 real<lower=0,upper=100> sigma_d;
}

transformed parameters {
vector[N] y_hat;

for (i in 1:N)
y_hat[i] <- beta[1] + beta[2] * class[i] + beta[3] * gender[i]
+ beta[4] * income[i] + beta[5] * lr[i]
+ beta[6] * age[i] + a[lag[i]]+ b[r0g[i]]+c[rgdp[i]]+d[cnt[i]];
}

model {
a ~ normal (0, sigma_a);
b ~ normal (0, sigma_b);
c ~ normal (0, sigma_c);
d ~ normal (0, sigma_d);

beta ~ normal(0, 100);
y ~ bernoulli_logit(y_hat);
}
'
micro.result <- stan(model_code=micro_code, data=dataList.1, iter=100, chains=2)

It looks mostly fine. The normal(0,100) priors are probably overly diffuse. You could use matrix algebra instead of that scalar thing for yhat. But the main thing I think is to declare and define yhat inside the model block rather than the transformed parameters block. All those yhats get written to the disk after the warmup period which involves a lot of I/O for something that you probably don't care about and could recreate in R if you did care about them.

Ben

