Hi Ben,

Thanks for your reply. I have taken your suggestions, but put the random effect u as a local variable in the model block, instead of a transformed parameter, so the sampling process would not need to store u every step. I have attached my new code below.

The speed has significantly increased with your trick, but I found it is still not satisfying. I found the matrix operation u<- sigmaU * (CK * z) really slow things down, in an order. If CK matrix was dropped in u <- sigmaU * (CK * z), it is much more quicker. I assume this is only an explicit assignment operation which will only be executed once each sampling step. So, why this takes so long, and are there ways to optimize this?

Thanks,

Here is the new code:
//-----------------------

data {
  int<lower=1> N;  // Sample number

  int<lower=1> K;  // Ad categories
  matrix[N,N] KS;  // Kinship
  int<lower=1,upper=K> Ad[N];  // Ad status

  int<lower=1,upper=2> Sex[N];  //  M:F

  vector[N] Age;  // Numerical

}

transformed data {
  matrix[N,N] CK;  // Cholesky K

  vector[N] sexF;  // Relative to M

  CK <- cholesky_decompose(KS);

  for (i in 1:N)

    sexF[i]  <- Sex[i]  == 2;
}

parameters {

  vector[N] z;  // primitive of ramdom effect
  real<lower=0> sigmaU;

  real pAge;  // Age

  real pSex;  // Female

  positive_ordered[K-1] cut;  // cut points
}

model {
  vector[N] yhat;

  vector[N] u;  // random effect

  pAge ~ normal(0, 5);

  pSex ~ normal(0, 5);
  sigmaU ~ normal(0, 5);

  z ~ normal(0, 1);

  u <- sigmaU * (CK * z);

  yhat <- u + Age * pAge + sexF * pSex;

  for (n in 1:N)
    Ad[n] ~ ordered_logistic(yhat[n], cut);
}

generated quantities {

  vector[N] u;  // random effect

  vector[N] yhat;
  vector[N] logLik;

  u <- sigmaU * (CK * z);

  yhat <- u + Age * pAge + sexF * pSex;

  for (n in 1:N)

    logLik[n] <- ordered_logistic_log(Ad[n], yhat[n], cut);
}
//-----------------------

